1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Nbd7 7. Qd2 e6 8. f3
b5 9. g4 h6 10. h4 b4 {[ A very rare move and an excellent idea to get White
out of main line preparation. Black usually plays 10..Bb7 11.0-0-0 b4 ]} 11.
Nce2 d5 12. exd5 (12. g5 hxg5 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Bxg5 Qa5 15. Nf4 Bb7 16. Bc4
Nxf4 17. Qxf4 Qe5+ $1 {[ Was good for Black in Yudasin-Gavrikov USSR Ch. Kiev
1986 ]}) 12... Nxd5 13. Bf2 $6 {[ I cant believe White has time for this, by
comparison with the main line 13.0-0-0 is better ]} Bb7 14. O-O-O Bc5 15. Ng3
Qa5 16. Bc4 N7b6 $5 {[ This works out well and was designed against 17.Nb3 16..
Ne5 fails to 17.Nb3]} (16... Ne5 17. Nb3 $1 (17. Bb3 O-O-O 18. Ne4 {[#] [ The
central box of knights ! ]} Nxf3 $1 19. Nxf3 Nc3 $1 $17) 17... Nxc4 18. Nxa5
Nxd2 19. Bxc5 $18) 17. Bb3 (17. Nb3 {[ Now this fails ]} Qxa2 18. Bxd5 Na4 $1
$19 {[ The point of Nb6 ]}) 17... Nc3 $2 {[#] [ Was this a bluff ? White now
caves in but what did Julian have in mind against 18.Nxe6! (?) ]} (17... O-O-O
$1) 18. Rde1 $2 {[ This just loses, White was perhaps a bit too overawed to
think about the advantage ! ]} (18. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 (18... Bxf2 19. Nc7+ (19. Rhe1
{[ D.Knox - looks incredibly strong ]}) 19... Kf8 20. Qxf2 Nxd1 21. Rxd1 $16
Rc8 22. Nf5 $1) (18... Nxa2+ 19. Bxa2 Qxa2 20. Nxg7+ $16 (20. Qd8+ Rxd8 21.
Rxd8+ Ke7 22. Bxc5+ Kxe6 23. Re1+ Kf6 24. Rd6+ {[ This line made the
commentators (birth) day ]})) 19. Bxc5 Qxc5 (19... Nxd1 20. Qd6 $18) (19...
Nxa2+ 20. Kb1 $1 (20. Bxa2 Qxa2 21. Qxb4 Qa1+ 22. Kd2 O-O-O+ $1 23. Ke2 Rxd1
24. Rxd1 Bxf3+ $1 $19) 20... Qxc5 21. Qd6 $1 (21. Bxe6 Nc3+ 22. bxc3 bxc3 23.
Bd7+ Kd8 $3 $19) (21. Bxa2 Bxf3 22. Rde1 $1 O-O $1 $13 (22... Bxh1 23. Rxe6+
$18) 23. Rxe6 Bd5) 21... Qxd6 22. Rxd6 Nd5 23. Re1 Nac3+ 24. bxc3 Nxc3+ 25. Kb2
O-O 26. Rexe6 Kh7 27. Nf5 $1 {[ /\ 27.Rxh6+ gxh6 28.Rxh6 # ]}) 20. bxc3 bxc3
21. Qd6 $16) 18... O-O-O 19. bxc3 bxc3 20. Qf4 Bxd4 21. Bxd4 Qa3+ 22. Kd1 Rxd4+
23. Qxd4 Bxf3+ 24. Ne2 Qb2 0-1

